+# Decision: Cache replacement policy for the preview worker
+
+## Context
+
+The preview worker renders one JPEG contact sheet per clip revision and renderer version and keeps them in a private 12 GiB cache. Editors work in two distinct modes: long sequential screenings that touch each sheet once, and comparison sessions that revisit a small set of sheets repeatedly. A replacement policy that follows recent activity well — such as least-recently-used — is the cheapest to run, but a long screening walks the cache end to end and pushes out exactly the comparison sheets an editor is working from. The policy must keep the cache within 12 GiB, must not change the `GET /internal/previews/{clip-id}/{revision}` contract or the render queue's four-job concurrency, and must keep its bookkeeping private to the worker, where no other node can read cache paths, counters, or queue state.
+
+The question at stake is what the worker keeps when it must choose. Getting it wrong does not corrupt anything; it makes editors re-render sheets they were comparing, at roughly 450 ms each.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Resistance to sequential screening scans** — *major*. A screening introduces one-use sheets at a steady rate; the sheets under comparison must survive it. This criterion carries the project's tenet to protect reused work over minimizing bookkeeping.
+- **Adaptation when editors switch projects** — *moderate*. When an editor moves to a different production, the sheets that were valuable a moment ago may be worthless, and the policy should not cling to them.
+- **Bookkeeping cost per cache operation** — *moderate*. Every request and every admission pays the policy's tracking cost on the worker's request path, alongside rendering.
+- **Cost of losing a cached comparison sheet** — *moderate*. A displaced comparison sheet is re-rendered on the next request, adding render latency to a session that is comparing several sheets at once.
+- **Implementation complexity** — *minor*. The worker has one cache and one policy; a policy that needs several tuned parameters is harder to configure correctly than one that does not.
+
+No gate eliminates any option: every policy considered leaves the HTTP contract, the render queue, the 12 GiB budget, and the privacy of the worker's bookkeeping untouched.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Least recently used
+
+- **Description** — Every cached sheet carries a timestamp of its last service. When the cache is full, the sheet served longest ago is evicted. A sheet is therefore protected only while nothing else is requested.
+- **Analysis**
+  - *Resistance to sequential screening scans* — **weak**. A screening requests each sheet once in sequence, so every sheet becomes the least recently served at some point during the walk, and the comparison set that preceded the screening is evicted by the scan itself.
+  - *Adaptation when editors switch projects* — **strong**. The policy has no memory beyond the last service, so a new project's requests immediately displace the previous project's sheets.
+  - *Bookkeeping cost per cache operation* — **strong**. One timestamp per sheet, updated on every hit, with no queues and no counters.
+  - *Cost of losing a cached comparison sheet* — **weak**. A screening displaces the comparison set at its own pace, and each displaced sheet is re-rendered when the editor returns to it.
+  - *Implementation complexity* — **strong**. A single ordering over the cached entries.
+- **Summary** — Cheapest policy to run, and the one most exposed to sequential scans.
+
+### Frequency-based eviction with aging
+
+- **Description** — Every cached sheet carries a reference count, incremented each time it is served. When the cache is full, the sheet with the lowest count is evicted. Counts decay or are periodically reset so that a clip that was popular last week does not hold cache space indefinitely.
+- **Analysis**
+  - *Resistance to sequential screening scans* — **strong**. A one-use sheet has a count of one and is the first candidate for eviction, so a screening displaces its own output rather than the comparison set.
+  - *Adaptation when editors switch projects* — **partial**. Popularity decays, but until it does, the previous project's favorites still outrank the new project's sheets, and the decay schedule has to be tuned to the rate at which projects actually change.
+  - *Bookkeeping cost per cache operation* — **weak**. Every hit updates a counter, and the aging mechanism adds periodic work over the whole cached set.
+  - *Cost of losing a cached comparison sheet* — **strong**. Frequently revisited sheets are exactly the ones kept.
+  - *Implementation complexity* — **partial**. Counts plus an aging pass, and a second parameter whose value determines how long yesterday's favorites survive.
+- **Summary** — Keeps genuinely popular sheets, at the cost of counters and a staleness window.
+
+### 2Q
+
+- **Description** — The cache is split into two queues and a bounded ghost list. The first-use queue holds sheets that have been served once, ordered by admission. The reuse queue holds sheets that have been served more than once, ordered by recency. A sheet served a second time is promoted from the first-use queue to the reuse queue. A sheet evicted from the reuse queue leaves its key behind in the ghost list; when such a key is requested again, the newly rendered sheet is admitted straight to the reuse queue instead of starting over. Each queue draws on its own share of the 12 GiB budget.
+- **Analysis**
+  - *Resistance to sequential screening scans* — **strong**. Screening output lands in the first-use queue, where it is the oldest admitted entry and the first to be evicted; comparison sheets sit in the reuse queue and are not touched while a scan runs.
+  - *Adaptation when editors switch projects* — **partial**. The ghost list is the weak point: a key that was protected in the previous production returns to the reuse queue on its next request, so the previous production's comparison set survives a project switch longer than a frequency count would keep it.
+  - *Bookkeeping cost per cache operation* — **partial**. The worker maintains two queue orderings and a bounded ghost list of keys on top of the per-sheet metadata every policy needs.
+  - *Cost of losing a cached comparison sheet* — **strong**. Comparison sheets stay in the reuse queue through a screening, and a sheet displaced from that queue returns to it when it is next requested.
+  - *Implementation complexity* — **weak**. Two queues, a ghost list, and a split of the 12 GiB budget between the queues, with a size split that has to be tuned against the real workload.
+- **Summary** — Protects the comparison set through a screening, at the price of a second queue, a ghost list, and a tuned split.
+
+## Selection
+
+2Q is chosen. It is the only option that is strong on the criterion that carries the tenet: while a screening walks through one-use sheets, those sheets enter and leave the first-use queue, and the sheets an editor is comparing stay in the reuse queue untouched. Least-recently-used loses exactly the comparison set in that situation, and frequency-based eviction reaches the same protection only by paying for counters and an aging schedule on every request.
+
+The decision turns on the tenet to protect reused work over minimizing bookkeeping, which sets the importance of scan resistance as major and lets the bookkeeping cost of 2Q be carried rather than optimized away. Least-recently-used is the stronger option on bookkeeping cost and implementation complexity, and it is the one the project would pick if editors screened footage without comparing. 2Q is chosen because the extra bookkeeping is affordable on one eight-core worker and because the alternative — an editor's comparison set vanishing mid-screening — costs render latency exactly when latency is most visible.
+
+Committing to 2Q forecloses the simpler policies for the pilot: the worker will not revisit least-recently-used or frequency aging without new evidence that the workload has changed. It obliges the worker to maintain a first-use queue, a reuse queue, and a ghost list of evicted reuse-queue keys, and it obliges the team to tune the split of the 12 GiB budget between the two queues against measured workload rather than by assumption. If the split proves wrong, it is a private worker setting and can be changed without touching the review API.
