-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+## Cache Replacement: 2Q
+
+The worker replaces cached sheets using 2Q. Two queues and a ghost list of keys separate sheets an editor has used once from sheets an editor keeps coming back to.
+
+**First-use queue** holds sheets that have been served exactly once since admission. It is ordered by admission time, and its oldest admitted sheet is evicted first. The delivery that returns a freshly rendered sheet counts as that sheet's first service, so the sheet stays in the first-use queue until the next request for it. A screening fills this queue with one-use sheets, and those sheets leave it before any repeatedly used sheet is touched.
+
+**Reuse queue** holds sheets that have been served more than once. It is ordered by recency of service, and its least recently served sheet is evicted first. A sheet served for the second time is promoted from the first-use queue into the reuse queue, where it becomes the most recently served entry, and is thereafter protected from scan traffic.
+
+**Ghost list** holds the keys of sheets that were evicted from the reuse queue, without their JPEG data. A key in the ghost list is evidence that the sheet was used more than once, so when a rendered sheet is admitted with a ghost-listed key, it goes to the reuse queue instead of restarting in the first-use queue. The ghost list is consulted at admission time, after the render completes, and the key leaves the ghost list once the sheet is admitted. The ghost list is bounded at 10,000 keys and drops its oldest recorded keys when full.
+
+The queues share the 12 GiB budget by size: the reuse queue may hold up to 9.6 GiB (80%) and the first-use queue up to 2.4 GiB (20%). The split is a private worker setting, chosen so that the protected working set has room for the bulk of the cache while screening traffic has room to run. Both caps are configuration, and their sum never exceeds 12 GiB, so holding each queue within its own cap keeps the cache as a whole within the 12 GiB limit.
+
+Eviction within a queue follows that queue's own order. A sheet is around 2 MiB, far below the first-use queue's cap, so no single preview can exceed its destination queue; a preview larger than the destination queue's cap is served to the requester without being cached. Promotion counts as an admission into the reuse queue: if a promoted sheet does not fit under the reuse queue's cap, the reuse queue's least recently served sheet is evicted to make room. A sheet evicted from the reuse queue has its key recorded in the ghost list; a sheet evicted from the first-use queue leaves no trace, so a later request for it starts over in the first-use queue.
+
+Replacement bookkeeping — queue membership, ordering, the ghost list, and the queue caps — stays inside the worker. The review API observes none of it and cannot request pinning, so the policy's cost is paid entirely by the worker.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache replacement policy | 2Q with two queues and a bounded ghost list | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | LRU follows recent work but a long screening evicts the comparison sheets. Frequency counting with aging retains popular clips but adds counter maintenance and can keep yesterday's favorites too long. 2Q keeps repeatedly used sheets in a protected queue while one-off screening scans are evicted first. The full reasoning is in `docs/intent/previews/decisions/cache-replacement-policy.md`. |
+| Split of the cache budget between the two queues | 80% reuse queue, 20% first-use queue | Even split; a single unsplit queue | The reuse queue holds the working set an editor compares against and needs the larger share; the first-use queue only needs room for screening traffic to run. |
+| Queue split as configuration rather than a constant | Configured byte caps per queue | Hard-coded constants | The workload mix varies by production; tuning the split is a private worker operation that does not touch the HTTP contract. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+
+1. ✅ Cache replacement policy is 2Q, with the reasoning recorded in `docs/intent/previews/decisions/cache-replacement-policy.md`.
+2. ✅ The budget split defaults to 80% reuse queue, 20% first-use queue.
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Whether the queue split and the ghost-list bound need adjustment after the pilot measures reuse rates on production footage.
+2. How the worker recovers its two queues and ghost list after a restart, given that the replacement metadata is held only inside the worker.
+
